window.BVX_INDEX=[{"u": "patients.html", "p": "Patients", "t": "Patients", "z": "pat", "k": "patients", "s": "For people with low vision or blindness: first steps after diagnosis, conditions, aids and technology, real experiences and where to get help.", "x": "For people with low vision or blindness: first steps after diagnosis, conditions, aids and technology, real experiences and where to get help. patient low vision blindness I have sight loss"}, {"u": "patients.html#brief", "p": "Patients", "t": "In a nutshell", "z": "pat", "k": "patients", "s": "Having low vision means you see poorly, even with glasses.", "x": "In a nutshell Having low vision means you see poorly, even with glasses. It does not mean you see nothing. Many conditions cannot be cured, but a lot can be done : aids, rehabilitation, technology and support. A low vision professional helps you choose aids and teaches you how to use them. It is fine to feel bad. Asking for psychological support early helps. You are not alone: there are associations and peer support groups. You can listen to any page with the Listen to this page button in the top bar. patient low vision blindness I have sight loss"}, {"u": "patients.html#sub-title", "p": "Patients", "t": "What you will find", "z": "pat", "k": "patients", "s": "I have just been diagnosed First steps, a basic plain-language glossary, myths and facts, and an honest guide to what can and cannot be done.", "x": "What you will find I have just been diagnosed First steps, a basic plain-language glossary, myths and facts, and an honest guide to what can and cannot be done. Understanding my condition The conditions that cause low vision, including rare diseases, and the types of loss: central, peripheral, contrast and light, and mixed vision. Optical aids, technology and apps Magnifiers, telescopes, selective filters, electronic aids, screen readers, braille displays, AI apps and research advances. Real experiences and peer support First-person video testimonials by condition and age, voices from the co-creation sessions and peer support groups to share experiences. Where to go and psychological support Directory of centres and organisations (CUV-UPC, B1+B2+B3, Fundació Punt de Vista, ONCE), low vision professionals, specialist psychologists, social work and rights. patient low vision blindness I have sight loss"}, {"u": "patients.html#urg-short", "p": "Patients", "t": "If you suddenly lose vision", "z": "pat", "k": "patients", "s": "Sudden vision loss, flashes, a curtain-like shadow or severe eye pain are an emergency: go to A&E or call 112 .", "x": "If you suddenly lose vision Sudden vision loss, flashes, a curtain-like shadow or severe eye pain are an emergency: go to A&E or call 112 . All warning signs . The content in this area is for guidance and is being reviewed with the clinical team and people with low vision. It does not replace seeing a professional. patient low vision blindness I have sight loss"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "I have just been diagnosed", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "First steps, a basic plain-language glossary, myths and facts, and an honest guide to what can and cannot be done.", "x": "First steps, a basic plain-language glossary, myths and facts, and an honest guide to what can and cannot be done. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#que-es", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "What low vision is", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "You have low vision when you see poorly and glasses, contact lenses, medicines or surgery cannot fully fix it.", "x": "What low vision is You have low vision when you see poorly and glasses, contact lenses, medicines or surgery cannot fully fix it. This makes some everyday things harder: reading, recognising faces, cooking, getting around outside or using your phone. Low vision does not mean blindness . Most people keep some sight, known as residual vision . With the right aids and training, it can be used much better. When sight is very reduced, people talk about legal blindness , even if the person still sees a little. This website is for you too if you are blind: it works with screen readers and braille displays. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#primers-passos", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "First steps", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "Understand your diagnosis Ask your ophthalmologist what condition you have, whether there is treatment, how it may progress and how often it should be checked.", "x": "First steps Understand your diagnosis Ask your ophthalmologist what condition you have, whether there is treatment, how it may progress and how often it should be checked. You can ask them to write it down or bring someone with you. Ask for a low vision assessment An optometrist specialising in low vision measures how you see in real situations and suggests aids for what you want to do: reading, cooking, watching TV, walking outside… Where to ask for one . Learn to use the aids Vision rehabilitation teaches you techniques (for example, looking “to the side” if you cannot see the centre) and supports you until you feel comfortable. Connect with others Associations and peer support groups put you in touch with people who have been through the same. Look after how you feel There is no need to wait until you feel very low before asking for psychological support . diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#glossari-basic", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "Words you will hear", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "A basic glossary.", "x": "Words you will hear A basic glossary. See the full glossary for more terms. Visual acuity How well you see fine detail. It is measured with letters that get smaller. Visual field All the space you can see without moving your eyes, including the sides. Residual vision The sight you still have and can make use of. Selective filters Tinted lenses that reduce glare and improve contrast. Assistive technology Technology for people with sight loss: screen readers, braille displays, electronic magnifiers… Vision rehabilitation Training to make better use of your sight and carry out daily activities more independently. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#mites", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "Myths and facts", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "Common misconceptions about low vision People say… The facts “Using my eyes a lot will wear them out.” No.", "x": "Myths and facts Common misconceptions about low vision People say… The facts “Using my eyes a lot will wear them out.” No. Reading close up, with a magnifier or close to the screen does not make the condition worse. If you get tired, rest. “Nothing more can be done.” Even when a condition has no cure, aids, rehabilitation, technology and support make daily life much better. “Anyone with a white cane is totally blind.” No. Many people with low vision see partly and use a cane only at night, in unfamiliar places or in poor light. “If she can read her phone, she sees fine.” You can have good central vision and a very narrow field at the same time, so you may trip or walk into doors. “With a positive attitude you can do anything.” Not quite. Some things will be harder or need doing differently. Saying so is not being negative. “Aids are for older people.” Aids and technology are useful at any age, including for study and work. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#que-es-pot-fer", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "What can and cannot be done", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "An honest guide, without discouragement or false promises.", "x": "What can and cannot be done An honest guide, without discouragement or false promises. You can Make better use of your sight with optical and electronic aids and filters. Keep reading, with a magnifier, large print, your phone or audio. Adapt your home to make it safer and more comfortable. Use your phone and computer with magnification or a screen reader. Get around more safely with orientation and mobility training. Keep studying, working and doing activities, with adjustments. It helps to know that Ordinary glasses cannot repair a damaged retina or optic nerve. Many conditions have no cure; treatment often slows them down. Some activities, such as driving, may no longer be possible if legal requirements are not met. Aids take time to learn: they can be tiring at first. Be wary of “miracle” treatments sold online. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#com-et-sents", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "How you feel", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "Losing sight can bring sadness, fear, anger or exhaustion.", "x": "How you feel Losing sight can bring sadness, fear, anger or exhaustion. This is a normal reaction. Talking helps: with family, with the professionals who care for you or with others going through the same. If you feel sad most days or do not feel like doing anything, ask for help. There are psychologists who specialise in sight loss. Some people with significant sight loss see things that are not there (shapes, patterns, people). This is called Charles Bonnet syndrome . It does not mean you have a mental health problem. Tell your eye care professional. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#urgencies", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "Important: when to go to A&E", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "Go to an emergency department straight away (or call 112 ) if you notice: sudden loss of vision, in one or both eyes; straight lines that suddenly look bent or wavy; flashes of light, many new floaters or a curtain-like ", "x": "Important: when to go to A&E Go to an emergency department straight away (or call 112 ) if you notice: sudden loss of vision, in one or both eyes; straight lines that suddenly look bent or wavy; flashes of light, many new floaters or a curtain-like shadow; severe eye pain with a red eye, especially with headache or nausea. If you have any of these signs and are given an appointment months away, insist: it is an emergency. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-diagnosis.html#preguntes", "p": "I have just been diagnosed", "t": "Frequently asked questions", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_diagnosis", "s": "Will I go blind?", "x": "Frequently asked questions Will I go blind? Most people with low vision do not lose all their sight. It depends on the cause and on each person. Ask your ophthalmologist how your condition may progress. Why don't ordinary glasses help enough? Glasses correct focusing problems, but they cannot repair a damaged retina or optic nerve. Low vision aids work differently: they make things bigger, increase contrast or reduce glare. Can I keep driving? Driving requires minimum vision standards. Your eye care professional can tell you whether you meet them. Do not drive if in doubt. Are aids expensive? Prices vary widely. Many tools are already free on your phone. Some organisations and public bodies offer grants for assistive products. Always try them before buying. diagnosis just diagnosed new recent first what now told me start myths"}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Understanding my condition", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "The conditions that cause low vision, including rare diseases, and the types of loss: central, peripheral, contrast and light, and mixed vision.", "x": "The conditions that cause low vision, including rare diseases, and the types of loss: central, peripheral, contrast and light, and mixed vision. condition disease symptom AMD glaucoma retinitis retinopathy myopia Leber Stargardt cornea transplant valve macular central peripheral contrast"}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#per-tipus", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "By type of sight loss", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "The same condition can affect people differently.", "x": "By type of sight loss The same condition can affect people differently. Knowing your type of sight loss matters as much as the name of the condition. You can see what these losses look like in the vision simulator . I cannot see the centre well Reading, recognising faces or seeing a screen is hard, but you can walk without tripping. Typical of AMD, Stargardt disease, macular holes and some optic nerve conditions. Helps: magnification (magnifiers, phone), more light and learning to look “to the side” (eccentric viewing). I cannot see the sides well You can read small print but bump into doors, trip, or do not see people approaching. Typical of advanced glaucoma, retinitis pigmentosa and some brain injuries. Helps: orientation and mobility training, a cane (often only at night) and good lighting. Light bothers me or I see poor contrast Light dazzles you (photophobia), things look washed out, or it is hard to tell a step from the floor. Happens with cataracts, corneal problems, albinism, retinal dystrophies and after some surgery. Helps: selective filters, a peaked cap, dark mode on screens and more contrast at home. Each eye sees differently, or it changes This is “mixed vision”: one eye sees far but without detail and the other hardly at all, or you see well by day and very poorly at night. Others do not always understand. Helps: a low vision assessment to make the most of each eye, and material to explain it to others . condition disease symptom AMD glaucoma retinitis retinopathy myopia Leber Stargardt cornea transplant valve macular central peripheral contrast"}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#per-malaltia", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "By condition", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "AMD Age-related macular degeneration affects central vision.", "x": "By condition AMD Age-related macular degeneration affects central vision. The wet form can be treated with injections if caught early; the dry form progresses slowly. If straight lines suddenly look wavy, go to A&E. Glaucoma Damages the optic nerve and usually starts at the edges, unnoticed. Treatment (drops, laser, surgery or drainage valves) slows it down. Severe eye pain with a red eye is an emergency, even if you have had surgery. Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion Diabetes and high blood pressure can damage the retina's blood vessels: vision becomes blurred, patchy or lacking detail. Controlling them and attending check-ups is the best protection. Retinitis pigmentosa Inherited. Often starts with poor night vision, then side vision narrows, like looking through a tube. A genetic test can confirm it. High myopia Very high short-sightedness that may come with damage to the macula, macular holes, retinal detachment, glaucoma or cataract. Regular check-ups are very important. Optic nerve conditions Inherited (such as Leber optic neuropathy), caused by a tumour pressing on the nerve (for example, a pituitary tumour), inflammation or poor blood supply. They usually affect central vision. Cornea and transplants Infections, dystrophies or injuries of the cornea can cause blurred vision and strong discomfort from light. After a transplant, ask about your outlook and what to expect. Cataract and intraocular lens A cataract clouds the lens and surgery usually works well. If the eye has other conditions, the improvement may be limited: ask beforehand. There are other  condition disease symptom AMD glaucoma retinitis retinopathy myopia Leber Stargardt cornea transplant valve macular central peripheral contrast"}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#minoritaries", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Rare diseases", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Many conditions that cause low vision are rare diseases : Leber hereditary optic neuropathy, Stargardt disease, Leber congenital amaurosis, choroideremia, achromatopsia, albinism or Usher syndrome, among others.", "x": "Rare diseases Many conditions that cause low vision are rare diseases : Leber hereditary optic neuropathy, Stargardt disease, Leber congenital amaurosis, choroideremia, achromatopsia, albinism or Usher syndrome, among others. Having an uncommon condition does not mean nothing can be done: low vision aids and rehabilitation work just as well. A genetic test can confirm the diagnosis, inform the family and open the door to clinical trials . Patient associations for a specific condition are a good source of information and contact with other families. condition disease symptom AMD glaucoma retinitis retinopathy myopia Leber Stargardt cornea transplant valve macular central peripheral contrast"}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#preguntar", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "What to ask at your appointment", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Bring these questions written down, or ask someone to come with you: What exactly is my condition called?", "x": "What to ask at your appointment Bring these questions written down, or ask someone to come with you: What exactly is my condition called? Could you write it down? Is there treatment? What can I expect over the coming months or years? How often should I be checked? Which signs mean I should come back sooner? Can you refer me for a low vision assessment and to social work? Are there associations or clinical trials for my condition? You can find experiences of others with the same condition in Real experiences and peer support . condition disease symptom AMD glaucoma retinitis retinopathy myopia Leber Stargardt cornea transplant valve macular central peripheral contrast"}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#central", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "I cannot see the centre well", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Reading, recognising faces or seeing a screen is hard, but you can walk without tripping.", "x": "I cannot see the centre well I cannot see the centre well Reading, recognising faces or seeing a screen is hard, but you can walk without tripping. Typical of AMD, Stargardt disease, macular holes and some optic nerve conditions. Helps: magnification (magnifiers, phone), more light and learning to look “to the side” (eccentric viewing)."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#periferica", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "I cannot see the sides well", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "You can read small print but bump into doors, trip, or do not see people approaching.", "x": "I cannot see the sides well I cannot see the sides well You can read small print but bump into doors, trip, or do not see people approaching. Typical of advanced glaucoma, retinitis pigmentosa and some brain injuries. Helps: orientation and mobility training, a cane (often only at night) and good lighting."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#contrast-llum", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Light bothers me or I see poor contrast", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Light dazzles you (photophobia), things look washed out, or it is hard to tell a step from the floor.", "x": "Light bothers me or I see poor contrast Light bothers me or I see poor contrast Light dazzles you (photophobia), things look washed out, or it is hard to tell a step from the floor. Happens with cataracts, corneal problems, albinism, retinal dystrophies and after some surgery. Helps: selective filters, a peaked cap, dark mode on screens and more contrast at home."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#visio-mixta", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Each eye sees differently, or it changes", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "This is “mixed vision”: one eye sees far but without detail and the other hardly at all, or you see well by day and very poorly at night.", "x": "Each eye sees differently, or it changes Each eye sees differently, or it changes This is “mixed vision”: one eye sees far but without detail and the other hardly at all, or you see well by day and very poorly at night. Others do not always understand. Helps: a low vision assessment to make the most of each eye, and material to explain it to others ."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#dmae", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "AMD", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Age-related macular degeneration affects central vision.", "x": "AMD AMD Age-related macular degeneration affects central vision. The wet form can be treated with injections if caught early; the dry form progresses slowly. If straight lines suddenly look wavy, go to A&E."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#glaucoma", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Glaucoma", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Damages the optic nerve and usually starts at the edges, unnoticed.", "x": "Glaucoma Glaucoma Damages the optic nerve and usually starts at the edges, unnoticed. Treatment (drops, laser, surgery or drainage valves) slows it down. Severe eye pain with a red eye is an emergency, even if you have had surgery."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#retinopatia", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Diabetes and high blood pressure can damage the retina's blood vessels: vision becomes blurred, patchy or lacking detail.", "x": "Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion Diabetes and high blood pressure can damage the retina's blood vessels: vision becomes blurred, patchy or lacking detail. Controlling them and attending check-ups is the best protection."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#retinosi", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Retinitis pigmentosa", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Inherited.", "x": "Retinitis pigmentosa Retinitis pigmentosa Inherited. Often starts with poor night vision, then side vision narrows, like looking through a tube. A genetic test can confirm it."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#miopia-magna", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "High myopia", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Very high short-sightedness that may come with damage to the macula, macular holes, retinal detachment, glaucoma or cataract.", "x": "High myopia High myopia Very high short-sightedness that may come with damage to the macula, macular holes, retinal detachment, glaucoma or cataract. Regular check-ups are very important."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#nervi-optic", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Optic nerve conditions", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Inherited (such as Leber optic neuropathy), caused by a tumour pressing on the nerve (for example, a pituitary tumour), inflammation or poor blood supply.", "x": "Optic nerve conditions Optic nerve conditions Inherited (such as Leber optic neuropathy), caused by a tumour pressing on the nerve (for example, a pituitary tumour), inflammation or poor blood supply. They usually affect central vision."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#cornia", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Cornea and transplants", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "Infections, dystrophies or injuries of the cornea can cause blurred vision and strong discomfort from light.", "x": "Cornea and transplants Cornea and transplants Infections, dystrophies or injuries of the cornea can cause blurred vision and strong discomfort from light. After a transplant, ask about your outlook and what to expect."}, {"u": "patients-conditions.html#cataracta", "p": "Understanding my condition", "t": "Cataract and intraocular lens", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_conditions", "s": "A cataract clouds the lens and surgery usually works well.", "x": "Cataract and intraocular lens Cataract and intraocular lens A cataract clouds the lens and surgery usually works well. If the eye has other conditions, the improvement may be limited: ask beforehand."}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "Magnifiers, telescopes, selective filters, electronic aids, screen readers, braille displays, AI apps and research advances.", "x": "Magnifiers, telescopes, selective filters, electronic aids, screen readers, braille displays, AI apps and research advances. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#principis", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Before you choose", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "Try before you buy.", "x": "Before you choose Try before you buy. A low vision professional will let you try several aids with your real tasks. Think about the goal. Reading letters is not the same as seeing the bus number or cooking. Allow time to learn. A magnifier can be tiring at first; with practice it gets easier. Review over time. If your sight changes, you may need a different aid. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#lupes", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Optical aids", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "Magnifiers Hand-held, stand or illuminated.", "x": "Optical aids Magnifiers Hand-held, stand or illuminated. For reading, labels or crafts. The stronger the magnification, the closer you hold it and the less text you see at once. Special glasses High-powered reading glasses (high addition) or prism glasses. They keep your hands free. Telescopes Small hand-held or spectacle-mounted telescopes for distance: signs, the board, bus numbers. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#filtres", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Selective filters", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "These are tinted lenses (yellow, orange, red or brown) that block part of the light , especially blue light.", "x": "Selective filters These are tinted lenses (yellow, orange, red or brown) that block part of the light , especially blue light. They reduce glare, improve contrast and help you adjust to changes in light when entering or leaving a building. There are different colours and strengths: they should be tried indoors and outdoors. Very useful for photophobia, retinitis pigmentosa, cataracts, corneal problems or after some surgery. They are not ordinary sunglasses: a low vision professional helps you choose. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#electroniques", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Electronic aids", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "Electronic magnifiers A camera and screen that enlarge text and let you change colours (for example, white or yellow text on black).", "x": "Electronic aids Electronic magnifiers A camera and screen that enlarge text and let you change colours (for example, white or yellow text on black). Portable and desktop models exist. Text-to-speech readers Devices or apps that photograph text and read it aloud: letters, recipes, labels. Books and audio Audiobooks, podcasts, large-print books and smart speakers to check the time or weather or make calls by voice. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#tiflotecnologia", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Screen readers and braille", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "Assistive technology lets you use phones and computers with little or no sight: Screen magnification and large text, built into phones and computers.", "x": "Screen readers and braille Assistive technology lets you use phones and computers with little or no sight: Screen magnification and large text, built into phones and computers. Screen readers , which read everything on screen aloud: VoiceOver (iPhone, iPad and Mac), TalkBack (Android), NVDA (Windows, free) and JAWS. Braille displays : a device that shows on-screen text in braille. If you use a public computer without these programs, this website has a Listen to this page button in the top bar. ONCE and associations offer courses, and some libraries have adapted computers. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#apps", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Apps for daily life", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "An indicative list of apps that participants use or recommended.", "x": "Apps for daily life An indicative list of apps that participants use or recommended. We do not endorse them and have not tested them on every phone; search for them in your phone's app store. Reviewed: November 2026. Useful apps for people with low vision or blindness What for Examples How it works Reading text and describing images with AI Seeing AI, Envision AI, Lookout (Android), Be My Eyes (Be My AI feature) Take a photo or point the camera and the app reads the text or describes what is there. Help from a volunteer Be My Eyes A sighted volunteer sees what your camera shows and explains it over a video call. Magnifying and improving contrast iPhone Magnifier, Android Magnifier Uses the camera as a magnifier, with light and colour filters. Getting around outdoors Lazarillo, Google Maps with voice guidance Tells you by voice where you are, the streets and nearby places. Phone accessibility VoiceOver, TalkBack, Select to Speak Read the screen aloud. Already installed. AI apps can make mistakes. Do not rely on them for important decisions, such as medication, without checking. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-aids-technology.html#avencos", "p": "Optical aids, technology and apps", "t": "Research advances", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_aids", "s": "Research is moving forward, and it is natural to feel hopeful.", "x": "Research advances Research is moving forward, and it is natural to feel hopeful. It is also important to know where things stand: Gene therapy: there is already an approved treatment for one retinal dystrophy caused by a specific gene, and many others are in trials. A genetic test is needed to know whether it could apply to you. AMD treatments: injections for wet AMD have been used for years; for dry AMD, new medicines are being studied and are approved in some countries. Leber optic neuropathy: there is a medicine approved in Europe and gene therapies under study. Retinal implants, stem cells and optogenetics: still experimental. Ask your ophthalmologist whether there are trials for your condition. You can search registered trials at ClinicalTrials.gov . Be wary of clinics charging for unapproved “miracle” treatments. aid magnifier filter glasses telescope electronic app application phone artificial intelligence AI screen reader braille VoiceOver TalkBack research advance trial treatment practical solution"}, {"u": "patients-experiences.html", "p": "Real experiences and peer support", "t": "Real experiences and peer support", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_stories", "s": "First-person video testimonials by condition and age, voices from the co-creation sessions and peer support groups to share experiences.", "x": "First-person video testimonials by condition and age, voices from the co-creation sessions and peer support groups to share experiences. testimonial experience video real person story peer support group share"}, {"u": "patients-experiences.html#testimonis", "p": "Real experiences and peer support", "t": "Video testimonials", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_stories", "s": "People with different conditions and ages explain how they experienced their diagnosis and how they have adapted.", "x": "Video testimonials People with different conditions and ages explain how they experienced their diagnosis and how they have adapted. All videos have captions, audio description and a full transcript. The first videos are being prepared. Participants are recording them with their informed consent. Each video will be published with captions, audio description and a full transcript, and you will be able to filter them by condition and by age. testimonial experience video real person story peer support group share"}, {"u": "patients-experiences.html#veus", "p": "Real experiences and peer support", "t": "What people told us in the sessions", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_stories", "s": "In November 2026, nine people with low vision or blindness took part in two co-creation sessions for this website.", "x": "What people told us in the sessions In November 2026, nine people with low vision or blindness took part in two co-creation sessions for this website. Here are some of the ideas they shared, summarised in our own words and anonymously: It took me many years to find out that optical aids existed. Nobody had told me about them. During the day I go without a cane and at night I need it. People don't understand and tell me they saw me without it. I can read small print, yet I walk into doors. People say I'm absent-minded. I don't want “you can do anything” messages. I want to know what I can do and how. Dark mode saves me; for someone else in the group it makes it impossible to see. We need to be able to choose. I wish I'd had a psychologist from the start, not only once I was already using a cane. testimonial experience video real person story peer support group share"}, {"u": "patients-experiences.html#ajuda-mutua", "p": "Real experiences and peer support", "t": "Peer support groups", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_stories", "s": "Sharing experiences with others is one of the most valued forms of support.", "x": "Peer support groups Sharing experiences with others is one of the most valued forms of support. There are formal groups run by organisations and informal ones. Association groups Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 organises activities, meetings and peer support. Condition-specific associations Many conditions (retinitis pigmentosa, glaucoma, AMD, rare diseases) have patient associations with groups and research news. Ask your professional or the association. Informal groups Messaging groups, social media or local meet-ups. Useful for everyday tips; always check medical information with a professional. ONCE If you are a member, ONCE also offers activities and groups. testimonial experience video real person story peer support group share"}, {"u": "patients-experiences.html#comparteix", "p": "Real experiences and peer support", "t": "Would you like to share your experience?", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_stories", "s": "If you would like to record a testimonial to help others, contact the University Vision Centre.", "x": "Would you like to share your experience? If you would like to record a testimonial to help others, contact the University Vision Centre. Always with your consent, and anonymously if you prefer. Contact the CUV testimonial experience video real person story peer support group share"}, {"u": "patients-where-to-go.html", "p": "Where to go and psychological support", "t": "Where to go and psychological support", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_support", "s": "Directory of centres and organisations (CUV-UPC, B1+B2+B3, Fundació Punt de Vista, ONCE), low vision professionals, specialist psychologists, social work and rights.", "x": "Directory of centres and organisations (CUV-UPC, B1+B2+B3, Fundació Punt de Vista, ONCE), low vision professionals, specialist psychologists, social work and rights. where to go directory centre association ONCE CUV psychologist psychology emotional support social work disability certificate rights paperwork benefits professional expert optometrist rehabilitation"}, {"u": "patients-where-to-go.html#directori", "p": "Where to go and psychological support", "t": "Directory of centres and organisations", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_support", "s": "University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Terrassa.", "x": "Directory of centres and organisations University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Terrassa. Specialist low vision unit: functional assessment, trial and fitting of optical and electronic aids, filters and training. Book an appointment at the CUV (in Catalan) Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 Peer support, activities, technology training and advocacy for the rights of people with visual impairment. Association website Fundació Punt de Vista Accessibility, inclusive design and content adaptation for people with visual impairment. Foundation website ONCE For people who meet the visual criteria for membership: rehabilitation, orientation and mobility, education, employment, assistive technology and psychosocial support. ONCE website where to go directory centre association ONCE CUV psychologist psychology emotional support social work disability certificate rights paperwork benefits professional expert optometrist rehabilitation"}, {"u": "patients-where-to-go.html#professionals-baixa-visio", "p": "Where to go and psychological support", "t": "Specialist professionals", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_support", "s": "These are the professionals who can support you.", "x": "Specialist professionals These are the professionals who can support you. You can ask your ophthalmologist or GP to refer you. Low vision optometrist: assesses how you see and fits aids and filters. Rehabilitation worker: trains you in reading, orientation and mobility, and daily living skills. Psychologist: supports you with the emotional impact of the diagnosis. Social worker: advises on benefits, paperwork and resources. We are preparing a validated directory of low vision specialists in Catalonia. Only professionals validated by the project team will be included. where to go directory centre association ONCE CUV psychologist psychology emotional support social work disability certificate rights paperwork benefits professional expert optometrist rehabilitation"}, {"u": "patients-where-to-go.html#psicologia", "p": "Where to go and psychological support", "t": "Psychological support", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_support", "s": "A diagnosis can stir up a lot: sadness, fear of the future, anger, a sense of loss.", "x": "Psychological support A diagnosis can stir up a lot: sadness, fear of the future, anger, a sense of loss. Families can struggle too. Asking for help is not a sign of weakness , and you do not need to wait until you use a cane or feel very low. Tell your GP: they can refer you to mental health services. Look for psychologists experienced in sight loss or chronic conditions. The Official College of Psychology of Catalonia has a directory of professionals. Associations and ONCE offer psychosocial support and groups. Talking to people going through the same helps: see the peer support groups . If you ever have thoughts of harming yourself, call 024 (Spain's free 24-hour suicide prevention line) or 112 . where to go directory centre association ONCE CUV psychologist psychology emotional support social work disability certificate rights paperwork benefits professional expert optometrist rehabilitation"}, {"u": "patients-where-to-go.html#drets", "p": "Where to go and psychological support", "t": "Rights, paperwork and social work", "z": "pat", "k": "pat_support", "s": "Disability certificate.", "x": "Rights, paperwork and social work Disability certificate. You can apply to the Government of Catalonia for an assessment of your degree of disability. It gives access to benefits and concessions. ONCE. If your sight is below specific limits (visual acuity of 0.1 or less, or a visual field of 10 degrees or less, in the better eye with correction), you can apply for membership. Social work. Your primary care centre (CAP) and local social services can advise on grants for assistive products, long-term care, transport and workplace adjustments. School and work. You are entitled to reasonable adjustments. See Schools and society . where to go directory centre association ONCE CUV psychologist psychology emotional support social work disability certificate rights paperwork benefits professional expert optometrist rehabilitation"}, {"u": "families.html", "p": "Family and community", "t": "Family and community", "z": "fam", "k": "families", "s": "For relatives, friends, schools and anyone interested: understanding low vision, supporting safely, adapting the home and building a more inclusive society.", "x": "For relatives, friends, schools and anyone interested: understanding low vision, supporting safely, adapting the home and building a more inclusive society. family relative friend partner son daughter father mother school society community carer"}, {"u": "families.html#brief", "p": "Family and community", "t": "The most important things", "z": "fam", "k": "families", "s": "Ask before helping.", "x": "The most important things Ask before helping. The person knows better than anyone what they need. Do not judge by what you see. Someone may read their phone and still walk into a door, or go without a cane by day and need it at night. Be clear and specific. “The cup is on your right” is better than “it's over there”. Do not overprotect. Doing everything for the other person is not always helping. Look after yourself. Supporting someone is tiring too. Ask for support when you need it. family relative friend partner son daughter father mother school society community carer"}, {"u": "families.html#sub-title", "p": "Family and community", "t": "What you will find", "z": "fam", "k": "families", "s": "Understanding low vision A simulator of how people with different conditions see, mixed vision and intermittent cane use, and how low vision differs from total blindness and other disabilities.", "x": "What you will find Understanding low vision A simulator of how people with different conditions see, mixed vision and intermittent cane use, and how low vision differs from total blindness and other disabilities. Supporting and guiding safely Short text and audio guides: offering your arm, warning about steps and doors, communicating well, not overprotecting and looking after yourself. Adapting the home and lighting A glossary of practical tips for the home: lighting, contrast, order, not moving things, kitchen, bathroom and safety. Schools and society Children and young people with low vision at school, “Inclusion of diversity” materials for schools, normalising the white cane and a more inclusive society. Guidance content under review by the project team and partner organisations. family relative friend partner son daughter father mother school society community carer"}, {"u": "families-understanding.html", "p": "Understanding low vision", "t": "Understanding low vision", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_understand", "s": "A simulator of how people with different conditions see, mixed vision and intermittent cane use, and how low vision differs from total blindness and other disabilities.", "x": "A simulator of how people with different conditions see, mixed vision and intermittent cane use, and how low vision differs from total blindness and other disabilities. understand how they see simulation simulator video mixed vision cane difference blindness invisible disability"}, {"u": "families-understanding.html#simulador", "p": "Understanding low vision", "t": "How they see: simulator", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_understand", "s": "Choose a type of sight loss to see an approximation of how the same scene looks.", "x": "How they see: simulator Choose a type of sight loss to see an approximation of how the same scene looks. Each option includes a written description of what can be seen. Choose how to see the scene Typical vision Central vision loss (for example, AMD or Stargardt disease) Peripheral vision loss or “tunnel vision” (for example, advanced glaucoma or retinitis pigmentosa) Blurred vision (for example, cataract or a damaged cornea) Reduced contrast sensitivity Glare and light sensitivity Patchy vision (for example, diabetic retinopathy) Simulation: A room: on the left a window and a person in a green jumper; in the centre a blue sign with the word “STOP”; on the right a wall clock showing three o'clock. Below, a table with a red bowl and a jar labelled “Salt”, and on the left a step marked with a yellow line. An approximate simulation for guidance only. Everyone sees differently, and the same person may see differently depending on light or tiredness. We are preparing simulation videos by condition, with audio description and a full transcript. understand how they see simulation simulator video mixed vision cane difference blindness invisible disability"}, {"u": "families-understanding.html#visio-mixta", "p": "Understanding low vision", "t": "Mixed vision and intermittent cane use", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_understand", "s": "One of the most frequent complaints in the co-creation sessions was being misunderstood by others.", "x": "Mixed vision and intermittent cane use One of the most frequent complaints in the co-creation sessions was being misunderstood by others. Some situations that may seem contradictory but are not: She reads her phone but walks into doors. She may keep central vision while having lost side vision. He doesn't say hello in the street. If he cannot see the centre, he may not recognise your face. It is not that he does not want to greet you. No cane by day, a cane at night. In poor light many people see much less. Using a cane only when needed is normal. One day they can do something, another day they can't. Vision changes with light, tiredness and mood. They are not exaggerating. Comments like “you're so absent-minded” or “I saw you without your cane” hurt. If in doubt, ask. understand how they see simulation simulator video mixed vision cane difference blindness invisible disability"}, {"u": "families-understanding.html#diferencies", "p": "Understanding low vision", "t": "Differences from other disabilities", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_understand", "s": "Low vision, blindness and other disabilities: what to bear in mind Situation What it means What helps Low vision Sees partly.", "x": "Differences from other disabilities Low vision, blindness and other disabilities: what to bear in mind Situation What it means What helps Low vision Sees partly. May read with aids, but with difficulty depending on light and distance. Contrast, good light, large print, patience and not moving things. Legal blindness Sight is very reduced, but often some perception of light, shapes or a very small field remains. Cane or guide dog, screen reader, braille, verbal descriptions. Total blindness No useful visual perception. Spoken or tactile information, braille, predictable surroundings. Visible disabilities (for example, wheelchair users) Others recognise the disability straight away. Physical accessibility. Low vision is often an invisible disability : because it does not show, the person constantly has to explain. Making that easier is a great help. understand how they see simulation simulator video mixed vision cane difference blindness invisible disability"}, {"u": "families-understanding.html#preguntar", "p": "Understanding low vision", "t": "Ask, don't assume", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_understand", "s": "Two people with the same condition may see very differently.", "x": "Ask, don't assume Two people with the same condition may see very differently. The best way to find out is to ask naturally: “What can you see well and what is hard?” “Would you like me to help, or would you rather do it yourself?” “How should I warn you about a step or an obstacle?” “What lighting works best for you?” Next: Supporting and guiding safely . understand how they see simulation simulator video mixed vision cane difference blindness invisible disability"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "Supporting and guiding safely", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Short text and audio guides: offering your arm, warning about steps and doors, communicating well, not overprotecting and looking after yourself.", "x": "Short text and audio guides: offering your arm, warning about steps and doors, communicating well, not overprotecting and looking after yourself. support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html#principis", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "The basics", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Ask before helping and how they would like to be helped.", "x": "The basics Ask before helping and how they would like to be helped. Say who you are when you arrive and tell them when you leave. Be specific: “on your right”, “two steps ahead”. Do not move things without saying so. Respect canes and guide dogs: do not touch or distract them. support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html#guia-vident", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "Guiding outdoors: offering your arm", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Offer your arm Ask if they would like help.", "x": "Guiding outdoors: offering your arm Offer your arm Ask if they would like help. If yes, lightly touch the back of their hand with your arm so they can find it. Do not grab or push them. They hold just above your elbow Walk half a step ahead at a comfortable pace. That way they feel your movements before you make them. Describe what is around Mention changes in the ground, kerbs, obstacles at head height and people approaching. Sitting down Guide their hand to the back of the chair and let them sit down themselves. support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html#escales", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "Steps, doors and narrow spaces", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Steps and stairs Stop before the first step and say whether it goes up or down.", "x": "Steps, doors and narrow spaces Steps and stairs Stop before the first step and say whether it goes up or down. Let them hold the handrail. Stay one step ahead and say when you reach the end. Doors Say whether the door opens towards you or away, and on which side. The person being guided can hold it as they go through. Narrow spaces Move your arm behind your back: this is the signal for them to step directly behind you. Return to normal afterwards. Public transport Point out the door, the step and the handrail. Say whether there are free seats and guide their hand to the back of one. support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html#comunicar", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "Communicating well", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Arriving and leaving Say your name when you arrive (“Hi, it's Martha”) and tell them when you leave, so they are not left talking to no one.", "x": "Communicating well Arriving and leaving Say your name when you arrive (“Hi, it's Martha”) and tell them when you leave, so they are not left talking to no one. Precise directions Use references like “on your left” or clock positions: “your glass is at three o'clock”. Avoid “here” and “there”. Describe without worry It is fine to say “look” or “see”. Describe what is happening: who has arrived, what is on the plate, what a sign says. If they also have hearing loss Face them, with light on your face, without shouting and somewhere quiet. Check they have understood. support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html#sobreproteccio", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "Helping without overprotecting", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Wanting to protect is natural, but doing everything for the other person takes away their confidence and independence.", "x": "Helping without overprotecting Wanting to protect is natural, but doing everything for the other person takes away their confidence and independence. Let them do things at their own pace, even if it takes longer. Help adapt the task (more light, markers, order) instead of doing it for them. Do not make decisions for them: ask. Encourage rehabilitation and learning technology: it will give them independence. support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-guiding.html#cuidar-se", "p": "Supporting and guiding safely", "t": "Caring for yourself to care for others", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_guide", "s": "Sight loss is a process of adjustment for the whole family.", "x": "Caring for yourself to care for others Sight loss is a process of adjustment for the whole family. There may be times of sadness, anger or denial. Listening and patience help a lot. You matter too. If you feel overwhelmed, seek support: family groups, associations or psychological services. See where to find psychological support . support guide arm step stairs door street sighted guide communicate talk overprotect independence carer self-care"}, {"u": "families-home-lighting.html", "p": "Adapting the home and lighting", "t": "Adapting the home and lighting", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_home", "s": "A glossary of practical tips for the home: lighting, contrast, order, not moving things, kitchen, bathroom and safety.", "x": "A glossary of practical tips for the home: lighting, contrast, order, not moving things, kitchen, bathroom and safety. home house adapt lighting light task lamp contrast order do not move things kitchen bathroom falls label marker practical tip"}, {"u": "families-home-lighting.html#llum", "p": "Adapting the home and lighting", "t": "Light", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_home", "s": "Even throughout the home, with no dark corners or sudden changes between rooms.", "x": "Light Even throughout the home, with no dark corners or sudden changes between rooms. Task lighting where they read, in the kitchen and bathroom, pointed at the task, not the eyes. No glare: sheer curtains or blinds to control sunlight, bulbs that are not seen directly, matt surfaces. Hallways and stairs well lit, with switches that are easy to find or motion sensors. If the person has photophobia , they may prefer less general light and more task light. Ask them. home house adapt lighting light task lamp contrast order do not move things kitchen bathroom falls label marker practical tip"}, {"u": "families-home-lighting.html#contrast", "p": "Adapting the home and lighting", "t": "Contrast", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_home", "s": "Contrasting tape on step edges.", "x": "Contrast Contrasting tape on step edges. Switches, sockets and handles that stand out from the wall. Dark plates for light food and light plates for dark food; light and dark chopping boards. Towels and mats in colours that contrast with the floor and walls. home house adapt lighting light task lamp contrast order do not move things kitchen bathroom falls label marker practical tip"}, {"u": "families-home-lighting.html#ordre", "p": "Adapting the home and lighting", "t": "Order and clear paths", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_home", "s": "Everything always in the same place.", "x": "Order and clear paths Everything always in the same place. If you must move something, say so. This is one of the most important tips. Remove rugs that cause trips, cables and objects from the floor. Doors and drawers fully open or fully closed, never half-way. Chairs always pushed in under the table. home house adapt lighting light task lamp contrast order do not move things kitchen bathroom falls label marker practical tip"}, {"u": "families-home-lighting.html#consells", "p": "Adapting the home and lighting", "t": "Glossary of practical tips", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_home", "s": "Tips sorted by place or activity.", "x": "Glossary of practical tips Tips sorted by place or activity. Bathroom Non-slip mat in the shower, grab rails if needed and products in different colours or with tactile markers. Appliance buttons Raised adhesive dots on the most-used settings of the oven, washing machine or microwave. Kitchen Under-cabinet lighting, utensils in contrasting colours and a fixed place for everything. Labels Large print, raised markers or elastic bands to tell jars, spices and cleaning products apart. Medicines Large pill organisers, clear labels and, if needed, apps that read the box. Phones and remotes Models with large buttons and, if needed, voice control. Front door A fixed spot for keys, cane and glasses; a light by the door. Clothes Organised by colour or with tactile markers; offer help matching outfits if the person wants it. home house adapt lighting light task lamp contrast order do not move things kitchen bathroom falls label marker practical tip"}, {"u": "families-home-lighting.html#gent-gran", "p": "Adapting the home and lighting", "t": "Older people", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_home", "s": "Sight loss increases the risk of falls.", "x": "Older people Sight loss increases the risk of falls. Check lighting and obstacles at home. Many older people with low vision see things that are not there. This is Charles Bonnet syndrome : it is not dementia. Talk about it openly and mention it to their eye care professional. Watch out for isolation: keeping up activities and contacts matters as much as adapting the home. home house adapt lighting light task lamp contrast order do not move things kitchen bathroom falls label marker practical tip"}, {"u": "families-schools-society.html", "p": "Schools and society", "t": "Schools and society", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_society", "s": "Children and young people with low vision at school, “Inclusion of diversity” materials for schools, normalising the white cane and a more inclusive society.", "x": "Children and young people with low vision at school, “Inclusion of diversity” materials for schools, normalising the white cane and a more inclusive society. school pupil student child son daughter young teacher education inclusion diversity white cane society work shop awareness campaign"}, {"u": "families-schools-society.html#escoles", "p": "Schools and society", "t": "Children and young people at school", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_society", "s": "Explore with your child how they see best: distance, light, print size.", "x": "Children and young people at school Explore with your child how they see best: distance, light, print size. Work with the school: teachers need to know their needs (sitting near the board or away from the window, enlarged or digital materials, extra time in exams, use of a tablet or magnifier). In Catalonia, pupils with visual impairment can receive support from specialist educational teams. Ask the school or ONCE. Encourage play, sport and activities with other children: independence is learned by doing. In adolescence, how they feel in front of others matters a lot. Talking openly with the class helps. school pupil student child son daughter young teacher education inclusion diversity white cane society work shop awareness campaign"}, {"u": "families-schools-society.html#inclusio", "p": "Schools and society", "t": "Inclusion of diversity: classroom materials", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_society", "s": "The project is preparing materials to work on low vision in primary and secondary schools, as part of the “Inclusion of diversity” strand.", "x": "Inclusion of diversity: classroom materials The project is preparing materials to work on low vision in primary and secondary schools, as part of the “Inclusion of diversity” strand. They will be based on this website's content and usable in a tutorial session. How do they see? An activity with the vision simulator to discover that there are many ways of seeing. In preparation Being a guide Practising sighted guide technique in pairs, blindfolded, in a safe space. In preparation Myths and facts A discussion based on the most common myths about low vision and canes. In preparation If you are a teacher and would like to help test these materials, contact the CUV . school pupil student child son daughter young teacher education inclusion diversity white cane society work shop awareness campaign"}, {"u": "families-schools-society.html#basto", "p": "Schools and society", "t": "The white cane: making it normal", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_society", "s": "The white cane is not only for people who are totally blind.", "x": "The white cane: making it normal The white cane is not only for people who are totally blind. Many people with low vision use one: only at night or in poor light; in unfamiliar or crowded places; to detect steps and obstacles they cannot see; so that others know they see poorly. Using it on and off is normal. For many people, starting to use a cane is a hard step: comments like “you didn't use one before” make it harder. Respecting it, not touching it and not questioning it is the best help. school pupil student child son daughter young teacher education inclusion diversity white cane society work shop awareness campaign"}, {"u": "families-schools-society.html#societat", "p": "Schools and society", "t": "A society that understands low vision", "z": "fam", "k": "fam_society", "s": "In the street and in shops Offer help without imposing it.", "x": "A society that understands low vision In the street and in shops Offer help without imposing it. Read prices or signs if asked. Say who you are when you speak. At work Screens with magnification or a screen reader, good lighting, accessible digital documents and flexibility. Many adjustments are simple and cheap. Online Accessible websites and apps, alternative text for images and audio-described videos benefit everyone. school pupil student child son daughter young teacher education inclusion diversity white cane society work shop awareness campaign"}, {"u": "professionals.html", "p": "Professionals", "t": "Professionals", "z": "pro", "k": "professionals", "s": "For ophthalmology, optometry, primary care (GPs) and social work: referral map, resource pack generator for consultations and low vision training.", "x": "For ophthalmology, optometry, primary care (GPs) and social work: referral map, resource pack generator for consultations and low vision training. professional ophthalmologist optometrist GP doctor primary care social work consultation refer patient"}, {"u": "professionals.html#brief", "p": "Professionals", "t": "Three key ideas", "z": "pro", "k": "professionals", "s": "Refer early.", "x": "Three key ideas Refer early. Vision rehabilitation should not be reserved for the most severe cases: the sooner, the better the adaptation. Communicate with empathy. Avoid “nothing more can be done” and always offer a concrete next step. Hand over resources. With the generator, you can prepare an accessible PDF with a QR code for your patient in one minute. professional ophthalmologist optometrist GP doctor primary care social work consultation refer patient"}, {"u": "professionals.html#sub-title", "p": "Professionals", "t": "What you will find", "z": "pro", "k": "professionals", "s": "Clinical and social referral map Indicative referral pathways to low vision services, rehabilitation, social work, psychology and associations for ophthalmology, optometry and primary care, and how to communicate the dia", "x": "What you will find Clinical and social referral map Indicative referral pathways to low vision services, rehabilitation, social work, psychology and associations for ophthalmology, optometry and primary care, and how to communicate the diagnosis. Resource pack generator for consultations Tick the resources you want to give your patient and instantly generate a single accessible PDF with a QR code that opens the same content online. Training and specialisation Classification of visual impairment, functional low vision assessment, reading list, specialist optometry training and contact with experts. This content is a first draft based on the project proposal, the CUV's clinical practice and the co-creation sessions. It will be validated with the clinical team, partner organisations and professionals in the field. professional ophthalmologist optometrist GP doctor primary care social work consultation refer patient"}, {"u": "professionals-referral.html", "p": "Clinical and social referral map", "t": "Clinical and social referral map", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_referral", "s": "Indicative referral pathways to low vision services, rehabilitation, social work, psychology and associations for ophthalmology, optometry and primary care, and how to communicate the diagnosis.", "x": "Indicative referral pathways to low vision services, rehabilitation, social work, psychology and associations for ophthalmology, optometry and primary care, and how to communicate the diagnosis. referral refer pathway protocol map primary care GP ophthalmologist optometrist social work breaking bad news communicate diagnosis criteria urgent"}, {"u": "professionals-referral.html#mapa", "p": "Clinical and social referral map", "t": "Referral map", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_referral", "s": "What to do depending on who detects the sight loss.", "x": "Referral map What to do depending on who detects the sight loss. In every case, give the patient a resource pack with a QR code . Ophthalmology When: diagnosis of a condition that leaves sight loss that cannot be fully corrected. Explain the diagnosis and outlook in plain language (see communicating the diagnosis ). Refer for a low vision assessment (specialist optometry, for example the CUV-UPC), without waiting for severe cases. Refer to social work for the disability certificate and benefits. Offer psychological support from the start. Inform about associations and, if criteria are met, ONCE . Primary care (CAP) When: a patient reports seeing less, tripping, having stopped reading, or has a risk condition (diabetes, hypertension, glaucoma). Rule out urgent signs : if present, same-day urgent referral to ophthalmology. If not urgent, refer to ophthalmology and manage risk factors. If already diagnosed, refer to low vision services, social work and mental health if needed. Assess fall risk and isolation, especially in older people. Optometry When: visual acuity or field does not improve with the best correction. If there is no diagnosis, refer to ophthalmology. Carry out or refer for a functional low vision assessment. Coordinate training with rehabilitation and inform about resources. Social work When: anyone whose sight loss affects daily life. Advise on the disability certificate, long-term care and grants for assistive products. Connect with associations, peer support groups and community resources. Identify isolation or vulnerability. referral refer pathway protocol map primary care GP ophthalmologist optometrist social work breaking bad news communicate diagnosis criteria urgent"}, {"u": "professionals-referral.html#mala-noticia", "p": "Clinical and social referral map", "t": "Communicating the diagnosis", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_referral", "s": "Participants remember for years how their diagnosis was given.", "x": "Communicating the diagnosis Participants remember for years how their diagnosis was given. Phrases like “nothing more can be done” or “with a retina like that, what do you expect?” leave patients with nowhere to go. Suggested good practice: Space and time: somewhere quiet, unhurried, offering to have a companion present. Find out what they know and want to know before explaining. Plain language and information in writing or an accessible format. Realism without hopelessness: explain what cannot be recovered and, above all, what can be done . A concrete next step: refer to professionals with more time and training (low vision optometry, social work, psychology) and hand over resources. Review at a later visit what they have understood and how they are. referral refer pathway protocol map primary care GP ophthalmologist optometrist social work breaking bad news communicate diagnosis criteria urgent"}, {"u": "professionals-referral.html#criteris", "p": "Clinical and social referral map", "t": "Criteria for low vision referral", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_referral", "s": "Visual criteria Visual acuity below 0.3 (6/18) in the better eye with best correction.", "x": "Criteria for low vision referral Visual criteria Visual acuity below 0.3 (6/18) in the better eye with best correction. Significant visual field loss or central scotomas. Reduced contrast sensitivity or disabling glare. Functional criteria Difficulty reading, writing, getting around or with daily living despite best correction. Loss of independence or giving up meaningful activities. Vision-related fall risk. Wellbeing criteria Anxiety, low mood or isolation related to sight loss. Need for guidance on resources, rights or benefits. Possible Charles Bonnet syndrome. Referral to vision rehabilitation should not be reserved for the most severe cases: early intervention eases adjustment. referral refer pathway protocol map primary care GP ophthalmologist optometrist social work breaking bad news communicate diagnosis criteria urgent"}, {"u": "professionals-referral.html#cap", "p": "Clinical and social referral map", "t": "Primary care: urgent signs", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_referral", "s": "Refer to ophthalmology the same day if there is: sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; new-onset metamorphopsia (distorted lines); photopsia, many new floaters or a curtain-like shadow; severe eye pain with a red eye, ", "x": "Primary care: urgent signs Refer to ophthalmology the same day if there is: sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; new-onset metamorphopsia (distorted lines); photopsia, many new floaters or a curtain-like shadow; severe eye pain with a red eye, headache, nausea or haloes (suspected acute intraocular pressure rise), including in patients who have had glaucoma surgery. An acute pressure crisis cannot wait three months just because “it isn't cancer”: optic nerve damage is irreversible. referral refer pathway protocol map primary care GP ophthalmologist optometrist social work breaking bad news communicate diagnosis criteria urgent"}, {"u": "professionals-referral.html#xarxa", "p": "Clinical and social referral map", "t": "Who does what", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_referral", "s": "Role of each service in low vision care Professional or service Main role Ophthalmology Diagnosis, medical and surgical treatment, monitoring, prognosis and referral.", "x": "Who does what Role of each service in low vision care Professional or service Main role Ophthalmology Diagnosis, medical and surgical treatment, monitoring, prognosis and referral. Low vision optometry Functional assessment, refraction, prescribing and fitting optical and electronic aids and filters, training. Vision rehabilitation Training in eccentric viewing, reading, orientation and mobility, daily living skills. Primary care and nursing Detection, risk factor control, falls prevention, early referral. Social work Disability certificate, benefits, long-term care and community resources. Psychology Support adjusting to sight loss, for patients and families. Education Adapting materials and the school environment. Associations and organisations Peer support, assistive technology training, activities and rights advocacy. referral refer pathway protocol map primary care GP ophthalmologist optometrist social work breaking bad news communicate diagnosis criteria urgent"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Tick the resources you want to give your patient and instantly generate a single accessible PDF with a QR code that opens the same content online.", "x": "Tick the resources you want to give your patient and instantly generate a single accessible PDF with a QR code that opens the same content online. generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#generador", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "1. Choose the resources", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Select all Clear all Understanding low vision What low vision is A simple explanation to help understand the diagnosis.", "x": "1. Choose the resources Select all Clear all Understanding low vision What low vision is A simple explanation to help understand the diagnosis. First steps after diagnosis What to do in the first weeks, step by step. Myths and facts Common misconceptions and what is actually true. Explaining the condition AMD (age-related macular degeneration) Affects central vision. Glaucoma Affects the optic nerve and side vision. Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion Damage to the blood vessels of the retina. Retinitis pigmentosa and other inherited retinal diseases Inherited, often rare, conditions. High (pathological) myopia Very high short-sightedness with risk of complications. Optic nerve conditions (including Leber's optic neuropathy) Central vision loss caused by nerve damage. Cornea, transplants and after surgery Blurred or distorted vision caused by the cornea. Practical advice Lighting and contrast at home Tips to see better without building work. Optical aids and filters Magnifiers, telescopes, special glasses and selective filters. Phones, apps and assistive technology Accessibility tools and useful apps. For families: how to help A short guide for the people around them. Contacts, support and rights Associations, CUV and ONCE Where to go in Catalonia. Psychological and emotional support For you and your family. Rights and paperwork Disability certificate, benefits and social work. Warning signs: when to go to A&amp;E When you must not wait. Personal note for the patient (optional) Only printed on the PDF. It is not stored anywhere or included in the QR code. Avoid  generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#preview", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Pack preview", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Baixa visió en xarxa · University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Resources prepared for you by your professional Date: Note from your professional: Scan this code with your phone to open these resources with large text, high co", "x": "Pack preview Baixa visió en xarxa · University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Resources prepared for you by your professional Date: Note from your professional: Scan this code with your phone to open these resources with large text, high contrast and read-aloud resource-pack.html The QR code will only work once the website is published on a domain (it is currently being opened from this computer). generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-bv", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "What low vision is", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "You have low vision when you see poorly and glasses, contact lenses, medicines or surgery cannot fully fix it.", "x": "What low vision is You have low vision when you see poorly and glasses, contact lenses, medicines or surgery cannot fully fix it. It does not mean blindness: there is almost always some useful sight left (your residual vision ). With aids and training, you can make much better use of it. Everyone sees differently: some people lose the centre, others the sides, and others see everything blurred or are very sensitive to light. More information on the website: I have just been diagnosed generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-dx", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "First steps after diagnosis", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Understand your condition.", "x": "First steps after diagnosis Understand your condition. Ask your ophthalmologist what you have, whether there is treatment and how often it should be checked. Ask for a low vision assessment. A specialist optometrist measures how you see in real life and suggests aids. Learn to use the aids through vision rehabilitation. Talk to others in the same situation: associations can put you in touch. Look after how you feel. If it is hard, ask for psychological support: there is no need to wait. More information on the website: I have just been diagnosed generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-my", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Myths and facts", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "“Using my eyes will wear them out.” No.", "x": "Myths and facts “Using my eyes will wear them out.” No. Reading close up or with a magnifier does not make the condition worse. “Nothing more can be done.” Not true: even when the condition cannot be cured, aids, rehabilitation and support make daily life much better. “If she uses a cane, she must be totally blind.” No. Many people with low vision see partly and use a cane only at night or in unfamiliar places. “With a positive attitude you can do everything.” Not that either. Some things will be harder or need doing differently, and it is fine to say so. More information on the website: I have just been diagnosed generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-amd", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "AMD (age-related macular degeneration)", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "AMD affects the macula , the part of the retina we use to read and see faces.", "x": "AMD (age-related macular degeneration) AMD affects the macula , the part of the retina we use to read and see faces. Side vision is kept, so people can usually walk around without tripping. There is a wet form, which may be treated with injections if caught early, and a dry form, which progresses slowly. If straight lines suddenly look wavy, go to A&E. Magnifiers, a task lamp and your phone help a lot with reading. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-gl", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Glaucoma", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Glaucoma damages the optic nerve .", "x": "Glaucoma Glaucoma damages the optic nerve . It usually starts at the edges of vision and is not noticed at first. Treatment (drops, laser, surgery or drainage valves) slows down the loss: never stop treatment without talking to your ophthalmologist. You may read well and still bump into objects or doors. Severe eye pain with a red eye, headache or nausea is an emergency : do not wait. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-dr", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Diabetes and high blood pressure can damage the blood vessels of the retina.", "x": "Diabetic retinopathy and retinal vein occlusion Diabetes and high blood pressure can damage the blood vessels of the retina. Vision may become blurred, patchy or lacking in detail. Good control of diabetes and blood pressure and regular check-ups are the best protection. Treatments (laser, injections) can slow it down. If each eye sees differently (“mixed vision”), a low vision professional can help you make the most of both. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-rp", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Retinitis pigmentosa and other inherited retinal diseases", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "These are inherited conditions.", "x": "Retinitis pigmentosa and other inherited retinal diseases These are inherited conditions. They often start with poor night vision, followed by narrowing of side vision, like looking through a tube. A genetic test can confirm the diagnosis and open the door to clinical trials. A white cane at night, good lighting and filters help a lot. Patient associations share information about research. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-hm", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "High (pathological) myopia", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Very high myopia can come with damage to the macula, retinal detachment, glaucoma or cataract.", "x": "High (pathological) myopia Very high myopia can come with damage to the macula, retinal detachment, glaucoma or cataract. Keep to your check-ups, even if your sight seems unchanged. Flashes, many new floaters or a curtain-like shadow are an emergency. Illuminated magnifiers, filters and electronic aids let you keep reading. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-on", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Optic nerve conditions (including Leber's optic neuropathy)", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "The optic nerve carries the image from the eye to the brain.", "x": "Optic nerve conditions (including Leber's optic neuropathy) The optic nerve carries the image from the eye to the brain. If it is damaged (by inheritance, as in Leber hereditary optic neuropathy , by a tumour pressing on it, by inflammation or by poor blood supply), central vision is usually affected. Many of these conditions are rare: ask for your case to be explained and whether treatments or trials exist. The remaining side vision can be used better with training. Screen readers and image-description apps help a lot. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-co", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Cornea, transplants and after surgery", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "When the cornea (the clear “window” at the front of the eye) is damaged or has been transplanted, vision may be blurred, with halos or strong discomfort from light.", "x": "Cornea, transplants and after surgery When the cornea (the clear “window” at the front of the eye) is damaged or has been transplanted, vision may be blurred, with halos or strong discomfort from light. Ask about the outlook in your case and what to expect over the coming months. Special contact lenses, filters and low vision aids can improve daily life. Keep to your treatment and check-ups to prevent transplant rejection. More information on the website: Understanding my condition generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-li", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Lighting and contrast at home", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Even lighting throughout the home and a task lamp for reading and cooking, pointed at the task, not at your eyes.", "x": "Lighting and contrast at home Even lighting throughout the home and a task lamp for reading and cooking, pointed at the task, not at your eyes. Avoid glare: sheer curtains, matt surfaces, a peaked cap outdoors. Contrast: dark plates for light food and vice versa; coloured tape on step edges and light switches. Order: everything in its place. People you live with should tell you if they move something. More information on the website: Adapting the home and lighting generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-ai", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Optical aids and filters", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Magnifiers , hand-held, stand or illuminated: for reading and close work.", "x": "Optical aids and filters Magnifiers , hand-held, stand or illuminated: for reading and close work. Small telescopes : to see signs or bus numbers. Selective filters: tinted lenses that reduce glare and improve contrast. Electronic magnifiers: enlarge and let you change colours. The best aid is the one that suits your vision and what you want to do. Always try it with a professional before buying. More information on the website: Optical aids, technology and apps generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-ap", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Phones, apps and assistive technology", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Phones already include magnification, large text, dark mode and a screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android).", "x": "Phones, apps and assistive technology Phones already include magnification, large text, dark mode and a screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android). There are AI-powered apps that read text, describe images or identify banknotes with the camera. People with little or no sight can use braille displays and speech output on a computer. Associations and ONCE run courses to learn how. More information on the website: Optical aids, technology and apps generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-fa", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "For families: how to help", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Ask before helping and how they prefer to be helped.", "x": "For families: how to help Ask before helping and how they prefer to be helped. Be specific: “the cup is on your right” is better than “it's over there”. To guide, offer your arm; they hold it just above the elbow. Warn them before steps and doors. Do not overprotect: doing everything for them takes away independence. Do not move things without saying so. More information on the website: Supporting and guiding safely generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-nw", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Associations, CUV and ONCE", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) , Terrassa: low vision assessment and rehabilitation.", "x": "Associations, CUV and ONCE University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) , Terrassa: low vision assessment and rehabilitation. Website: cuv.upc.edu Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 : peer support, activities and rights advocacy. Website: b1b2b3.org Fundació Punt de Vista : accessibility and content adaptation. ONCE (Spanish National Organisation of the Blind): if you meet the visual criteria for membership, rehabilitation, education, employment and assistive technology services. Website: once.es More information on the website: Where to go and psychological support generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-ps", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Psychological and emotional support", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Losing sight can bring sadness, fear, anger or exhaustion.", "x": "Psychological and emotional support Losing sight can bring sadness, fear, anger or exhaustion. This is a normal reaction to a major change. There is no need to wait until you feel very low or use a cane before asking for help. There are psychologists experienced in visual impairment; ask your GP, the associations or ONCE. Talking to others in the same situation (peer support groups) helps a lot. More information on the website: Where to go and psychological support generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-rg", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Rights and paperwork", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Disability certificate: you can apply to the Government of Catalonia.", "x": "Rights and paperwork Disability certificate: you can apply to the Government of Catalonia. It gives access to benefits and concessions. Social work: your primary care centre (CAP) or your local social services can advise on benefits, long-term care and resources. ONCE: membership if visual acuity is 0.1 or less, or the visual field is 10 degrees or less, in the better eye with correction. More information on the website: Where to go and psychological support generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#sh-ur", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "Warning signs: when to go to A&amp;E", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Go to A&E straight away or call 112 if you notice: sudden loss of vision in one or both eyes; straight lines that suddenly look bent or wavy; flashes of light, many new floaters or a curtain-like shadow; severe eye pain ", "x": "Warning signs: when to go to A&amp;E Go to A&E straight away or call 112 if you notice: sudden loss of vision in one or both eyes; straight lines that suddenly look bent or wavy; flashes of light, many new floaters or a curtain-like shadow; severe eye pain with a red eye, especially with headache or nausea. If you are told to wait months and you have any of these signs, insist. More information on the website: I have just been diagnosed This information is for guidance and does not replace seeing a professional. If you notice sudden vision loss, go to A&E or call 112. generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-resource-pack.html#com-funciona", "p": "Resource pack generator for consultations", "t": "How it works", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_generator", "s": "Tick the resources.", "x": "How it works Tick the resources. The preview updates instantly. Press Create the PDF . The browser's print window will open: choose Save as PDF to get the file, or print it directly. Give it to the patient or send them the file. The QR code opens the same resources online, where they can enlarge text, change contrast or listen. Accessibility: the PDF keeps the heading and list structure (current browsers produce tagged PDFs), using the Atkinson Hyperlegible typeface at a large size. Privacy: the generator runs in your browser. No patient data is sent or stored. generator resources checkboxes PDF QR code print hand out patient consultation leaflet pack kit"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Training and specialisation", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "Classification of visual impairment, functional low vision assessment, reading list, specialist optometry training and contact with experts.", "x": "Classification of visual impairment, functional low vision assessment, reading list, specialist optometry training and contact with experts. training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html#classificacio", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Classification of visual impairment", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "The WHO International Classification of Diseases (ICD-11) classifies distance visual impairment by presenting visual acuity in the better eye: Categories of distance visual impairment (ICD-11, WHO) Category Visual acuity", "x": "Classification of visual impairment The WHO International Classification of Diseases (ICD-11) classifies distance visual impairment by presenting visual acuity in the better eye: Categories of distance visual impairment (ICD-11, WHO) Category Visual acuity worse than Visual acuity equal to or better than Mild 6/12 (0.5 decimal) 6/18 (0.3) Moderate 6/18 (0.3) 6/60 (0.1) Severe 6/60 (0.1) 3/60 (0.05) Blindness 3/60 (0.05) — Near visual impairment is defined as near visual acuity worse than N6 (0.8 M) with existing correction. Visual acuity does not describe all visual function: visual field, contrast sensitivity, glare and the person's needs are equally decisive. training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html#avaluacio", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Functional low vision assessment", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "The aim is not just to measure, but to understand what the person wants to do and what prevents them: Functional history and goals Diagnosis and progression, treatments, activities given up, environment, digital skills, ", "x": "Functional low vision assessment The aim is not just to measure, but to understand what the person wants to do and what prevents them: Functional history and goals Diagnosis and progression, treatments, activities given up, environment, digital skills, social support and expectations. Prioritise concrete goals. Distance and near visual acuity With low vision charts (logMAR, e.g. ETDRS type) at reduced distances if needed. Record reading acuity and reading speed. Refraction Careful refraction with adapted techniques often brings improvements that had been missed. Contrast sensitivity and glare For example with the Pelli-Robson chart. Assess response to different lighting levels and the value of selective filters. Visual field and scotomas Peripheral and central fields (Amsler grid, perimetry), locating scotomas and the preferred retinal locus to guide eccentric viewing training. Magnification prediction and trial of aids Initial magnification estimate (e.g. Kestenbaum's rule: addition in dioptres ≈ reciprocal of decimal distance acuity) and real-world trial of optical, electronic and technological aids against goals. Training, follow-up and referral Training plan, lighting and environment advice, and coordination with rehabilitation, social work, psychology and associations. training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html#comunicacio", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Accessible communication", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "Introduce yourself by name on entering and say when you leave.", "x": "Accessible communication Introduce yourself by name on entering and say when you leave. Address the person, not their companion. Describe what you are doing during the examination and before touching. Provide information in large print (at least 16–18 pt, sans serif, good contrast) or an accessible digital format, such as the resource pack generator . Many older people with low vision also have hearing loss: reduce noise, face them, with light on your face. training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html#bibliografia", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Reading list", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "World Health Organization.", "x": "Reading list World Health Organization. World report on vision . Geneva: WHO; 2019. World Health Organization. Blindness and vision impairment: fact sheet . World Health Organization. International Classification of Diseases, 11th revision (ICD-11) : category 9D90, vision impairment. Pelli DG, Robson JG, Wilkins AJ. The design of a new letter chart for measuring contrast sensitivity. Clinical Vision Sciences . 1988;2(3):187-199. American Academy of Ophthalmology. Vision Rehabilitation Preferred Practice Pattern . Initial reading list, to be expanded and validated by the project's clinical team. training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html#formacio", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Training and knowledge transfer", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "University training The Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (UPC) offers undergraduate and postgraduate optometry training, including low vision content and placements at the CUV.", "x": "Training and knowledge transfer University training The Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (UPC) offers undergraduate and postgraduate optometry training, including low vision content and placements at the CUV. FOOT-UPC website Workshops and talks In-person and online sessions for eye care, primary care and social work professionals. Planned for phase 3 Transfer materials Guides, summaries and downloadable resources in accessible formats. Planned for phase 3 OPTOM congress Presentation of the project, the co-creation methodology and first results. Planned training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "professionals-training.html#experts", "p": "Training and specialisation", "t": "Contact with experts", "z": "pro", "k": "pro_training", "s": "For case queries, training or collaboration, contact the low vision unit at the University Vision Centre.", "x": "Contact with experts For case queries, training or collaboration, contact the low vision unit at the University Vision Centre. You can also take part in co-creation sessions and content validation. Contact the CUV training course specialisation bibliography reading list article guideline classification ICD-11 acuity functional assessment Kestenbaum Pelli-Robson expert contact optometry"}, {"u": "about-us.html", "p": "About us", "t": "About us", "z": "", "k": "about", "s": "Baixa visió en xarxa is an alliance between the University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC), Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 and Fundació Punt de Vista.", "x": "Baixa visió en xarxa is an alliance between the University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC), Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 and Fundació Punt de Vista. about us who we are alliance organisations CUV UPC B1+B2+B3 Punt de Vista COOOC team project contact"}, {"u": "about-us.html#alianca", "p": "About us", "t": "The organisations", "z": "", "k": "about", "s": "University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Coordinator.", "x": "The organisations University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Coordinator. University health centre of the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (UPC), with a specialist low vision unit. Principal investigator: Prof. Lluís Pérez Mañá. CUV website Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 Partner. Defends the rights and inclusion of people with visual impairment and contributes their direct experience. Association website Fundació Punt de Vista Partner. Accessibility, inclusive design and content adaptation for people with visual impairment. Foundation website Official College of Opticians and Optometrists of Catalonia (COOOC) Collaborator. The professional body for optics and optometry in Catalonia. It hosted the November 2026 co-creation sessions at its headquarters. COOOC website about us who we are alliance organisations CUV UPC B1+B2+B3 Punt de Vista COOOC team project contact"}, {"u": "about-us.html#mes", "p": "About us", "t": "More about us", "z": "", "k": "about", "s": "The project Goals, phases, co-creation sessions, methodology and expected impact.", "x": "More about us The project Goals, phases, co-creation sessions, methodology and expected impact. Team The people who make the project possible. Take part in user testing Try the website and tell us what needs improving before it opens to everyone. about us who we are alliance organisations CUV UPC B1+B2+B3 Punt de Vista COOOC team project contact"}, {"u": "about-us.html#contacte", "p": "About us", "t": "Contact", "z": "", "k": "about", "s": "For any questions about the project, contact the University Vision Centre.", "x": "Contact For any questions about the project, contact the University Vision Centre. Contact the CUV about us who we are alliance organisations CUV UPC B1+B2+B3 Punt de Vista COOOC team project contact"}, {"u": "project.html", "p": "The project", "t": "The project", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "Goals, phases, co-creation methodology, expected impact and partners of the Baixa visió en xarxa project, coordinated by the UPC University Vision Centre.", "x": "Goals, phases, co-creation methodology, expected impact and partners of the Baixa visió en xarxa project, coordinated by the UPC University Vision Centre. "}, {"u": "project.html#repte", "p": "The project", "t": "The challenge", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "Low vision is a health, inclusion and equity challenge that is growing as the population ages.", "x": "The challenge Low vision is a health, inclusion and equity challenge that is growing as the population ages. Beyond the clinical condition, people with low vision struggle to access reliable, accessible information, suitable support resources and ongoing contact with specialist professionals. These difficulties are made worse by fragmented resources , a lack of accessible and validated digital solutions, and the gap between academic knowledge, professional practice and society. "}, {"u": "project.html#objectiu", "p": "The project", "t": "Goal", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "To develop, validate and transfer an accessible digital platform that turns specialist knowledge about low vision into a usable, structured and socially relevant resource, able to improve the independence of people with ", "x": "Goal To develop, validate and transfer an accessible digital platform that turns specialist knowledge about low vision into a usable, structured and socially relevant resource, able to improve the independence of people with low vision and strengthen professional practice in eye health. "}, {"u": "project.html#fases", "p": "The project", "t": "Phases and timeline", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "The project runs for 18 months in three consecutive phases, plus a cross-cutting management and communication phase.", "x": "Phases and timeline The project runs for 18 months in three consecutive phases, plus a cross-cutting management and communication phase. Phase 0 · Launch and project management Months 1 to 18 Management structure, roles, communication channels, dissemination plan and participation mechanisms for partner organisations. Phase 1 · Needs analysis, co-creation and functional definition Months 1 to 6 Needs analysis with a diverse range of users and professionals, co-creation sessions and functional definition of the platform. Deliverables: needs analysis report; functional definition document. Phase 2 · Design and development of the accessible digital solution Months 7 to 14 Information architecture, accessible technical development, plain-language content creation and iterative validation. Deliverables: functional, accessible platform; structured content; functional validation report. Phase 3 · Validation, use and knowledge transfer Months 15 to 18 Real-world validation with people with low vision, final adjustments, documentation of lessons learned, and transfer and dissemination activities. Deliverables: validated platform; transfer and dissemination materials; lessons-learned report; final report. "}, {"u": "project.html#cocreacio", "p": "The project", "t": "Co-creation sessions (November 2026)", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "On 19 and 20 November 2026, at the headquarters of the Official College of Opticians and Optometrists of Catalonia (COOOC), two focus group sessions were held with 9 people with very diverse profiles: from legal blindnes", "x": "Co-creation sessions (November 2026) On 19 and 20 November 2026, at the headquarters of the Official College of Opticians and Optometrists of Catalonia (COOOC), two focus group sessions were held with 9 people with very diverse profiles: from legal blindness with intensive use of screen readers and braille displays to functional low vision, photophobia, central or peripheral field loss and complications after surgery. Their conclusions, together with the principal investigator's strategic priorities, have shaped the current structure of this website. Four principles Universal, customisable accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA/AAA): compatibility with screen readers and braille displays, and two-way contrast (dark mode and light high-contrast mode), because a single mode disadvantages some people. Three main areas and an assistant (two-click rule): Patients; Family and community; Professionals. Realistic empathy: neither defeatist messages nor toxic positivity; real testimonials, myths and facts, and what can be done. Active clinical referral: downloadable tools with checkboxes and QR codes for professionals to hand out in consultations. What we learned The post-diagnosis gap: some people took between 2 and 38 years to learn about optical aids and rehabilitation. Lack of sensitivity in some consultations and late referrals from primary care. Misunderstanding of mixed vision and intermittent cane use. Rejection of toxic positivity: people asked for a warm, hopeful and realistic tone. Opposite visual needs: some people need a dark background and others a light one, and text must be enla "}, {"u": "project.html#metodologia", "p": "The project", "t": "How we work", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "Co-creation People with low vision, professionals and organisations take part from the start in defining needs, content and features.", "x": "How we work Co-creation People with low vision, professionals and organisations take part from the start in defining needs, content and features. Iterative validation Every stage is tested with users to find barriers and fix them early. Catalan as source language Sessions, documents and content are created in Catalan, in plain and easy-read language, and reviewed by the UPC Language and Terminology Service. Gender perspective and inclusion Participants diverse in gender, age, level of sight and digital skills, with attention to socio-economic and digital inequalities. Sustainability Lightweight digital architecture, open standards, focus on essential features and fewer printed materials. Open science Publicly accessible content and materials, anonymised and aggregated data, and shared lessons learned to encourage reuse. "}, {"u": "project.html#impacte", "p": "The project", "t": "Expected impact", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "People with low vision Greater independence in daily life.", "x": "Expected impact People with low vision Greater independence in daily life. Better ability to make informed decisions about eye health. Fewer barriers to specialist information. Better perceived quality of life. Professionals Access to training resources and structured materials. Better coordination between health and social care. More consistent support for people with low vision. System and society A replicable model of knowledge transfer in eye health. Content that can be reused and adapted to other regions. More awareness and less stigma. Impact will be measured with indicators on platform use, satisfaction among users and professionals, perceived independence (before and after), transfer activities carried out and use of content in Catalan. "}, {"u": "project.html#ods", "p": "The project", "t": "Sustainable Development Goals", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "SDG 3 Good health and well-being Main contribution, especially to target 3.4: emotional well-being, less uncertainty and stronger links with reference services.", "x": "Sustainable Development Goals SDG 3 Good health and well-being Main contribution, especially to target 3.4: emotional well-being, less uncertainty and stronger links with reference services. SDG 10 Reduced inequalities Tackling barriers linked to visual impairment, the digital divide and other forms of inequality. SDG 4 Quality education Access to adapted knowledge and lifelong learning for users and professionals. "}, {"u": "project.html#entitats", "p": "The project", "t": "Organisations", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Coordinating organisation.", "x": "Organisations University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Coordinating organisation. A university eye-care centre providing clinical care, teaching and research, with a specialist low vision unit. CUV website Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 Partner organisation. Works for the rights, social inclusion and quality of life of people with visual impairment. Brings the lived experience of people with low vision. Association website Fundació Punt de Vista Partner organisation. Specialises in accessibility, inclusive design and content adaptation. Helps define criteria, validate the platform's accessibility and disseminate results. Foundation website Official College of Opticians and Optometrists of Catalonia (COOOC) Collaborator. The professional body for optics and optometry in Catalonia. It hosted the November 2026 co-creation sessions at its headquarters. COOOC website AGAUR Project submitted to the call for projects with knowledge impact on society (IMPACTE 2025) of the Catalan Agency for Management of University and Research Grants. AGAUR website "}, {"u": "project.html#trajectoria", "p": "The project", "t": "Track record", "z": "", "k": "project", "s": "The project builds on the service-learning work in eye health of the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (FOOT) through the CUV.", "x": "Track record The project builds on the service-learning work in eye health of the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (FOOT) through the CUV. Since 2007, the Mirades Solidàries programme has provided eye care to more than 40,000 people across Catalonia, over 30,000 of them in vulnerable situations, working with around 70 local authorities and third-sector organisations. The CUV also coordinated the project “ Aprenentatge i servei en gestió de la miopia ” (service-learning in myopia management), funded under the AGAUR IMPACTE 2023 call. "}, {"u": "team.html", "p": "Team", "t": "Team", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "The multidisciplinary team behind Baixa visió en xarxa: scientific leadership, digital accessibility, clinical experts, education, communication and institutional support.", "x": "The multidisciplinary team behind Baixa visió en xarxa: scientific leadership, digital accessibility, clinical experts, education, communication and institutional support. "}, {"u": "team.html#g-direccio", "p": "Team", "t": "Scientific leadership and coordination", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "Day-to-day coordination is led by the University Vision Centre (CUV), which ensures the coherence of all actions, monitors the work plan and integrates the contributions of the whole team.", "x": "Scientific leadership and coordination Day-to-day coordination is led by the University Vision Centre (CUV), which ensures the coherence of all actions, monitors the work plan and integrates the contributions of the whole team. Dr. Lluís Pérez Mañá Principal investigator Lecturer · Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (UPC) · CUV Optometrist specialising in low vision and visual rehabilitation, with more than fifteen years of clinical experience with people with low vision and an established track record in university research and teaching. He leads the project scientifically and oversees the suitability of its content. FUTUR profile (UPC) CUV staff "}, {"u": "team.html#g-tecnic", "p": "Team", "t": "Technical and digital accessibility team", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "AM Antonio Moisés Funes Martín Digital accessibility and assistive technology expert Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 He has in-depth knowledge of the WCAG standards and of good practice in accessible c", "x": "Technical and digital accessibility team AM Antonio Moisés Funes Martín Digital accessibility and assistive technology expert Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 He has in-depth knowledge of the WCAG standards and of good practice in accessible content. He works as an employment counsellor and assistive technology specialist, and handles accessibility requests at the association. His input is key to making the platform genuinely usable. B1+B2+B3 association website "}, {"u": "team.html#g-clinic", "p": "Team", "t": "Clinical and eye-health experts", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "Professionals with proven experience in low vision, visual rehabilitation and vision therapy, who help create and validate the content.", "x": "Clinical and eye-health experts Professionals with proven experience in low vision, visual rehabilitation and vision therapy, who help create and validate the content. CO Carolina Ortiz Herrera Low vision specialist Optometrist · University of Granada Lecturer at the University of Granada, specialising in low vision. University of Granada profile AP Adrián Pérez Baladrón Low vision and visual rehabilitation specialist Optometrist · University of Santiago de Compostela Associate lecturer at the University of Santiago de Compostela, specialising in low vision and visual rehabilitation. Universidade de Santiago de Compostela BS Bernat Sunyer Grau Vision therapy and visual rehabilitation Optometrist · Lecturer, FOOT (UPC) Lecturer at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa, specialising in vision therapy and visual rehabilitation. FUTUR profile (UPC) Núria Tomàs Corominas Optical instruments and accessibility Optometrist · Associate professor, FOOT (UPC) Associate professor at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa, specialising in optical instruments and accessibility. Academic profile (UPC) Dr. Marc Argilés Sans Vision therapy, visual rehabilitation and data analysis Optometrist · Lecturer, FOOT (UPC) Lecturer at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa, specialising in vision therapy, visual rehabilitation and data analysis. FUTUR profile (UPC) Dr. Ignasi Jürgens Ophthalmologist, retina specialist Medical director · Institut Català de Retina (ICR) Ophthalmologist specialising in the retina and vitreoretinal disease, medical director of the Institut Ca "}, {"u": "team.html#g-educacio", "p": "Team", "t": "Education, rehabilitation and content adaptation", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "LG Leonela González Vides Vision rehabilitation specialist Specialises in the education of children with visual impairment.", "x": "Education, rehabilitation and content adaptation LG Leonela González Vides Vision rehabilitation specialist Specialises in the education of children with visual impairment. She helps adapt materials to different levels of understanding and learning needs. "}, {"u": "team.html#g-comunicacio", "p": "Team", "t": "Communication, outreach and knowledge transfer", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "ML Montserrat Lucena Communication and digital marketing Optometrist · Associate lecturer, FOOT (UPC) Associate lecturer at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa, specialising in communication and digital marke", "x": "Communication, outreach and knowledge transfer ML Montserrat Lucena Communication and digital marketing Optometrist · Associate lecturer, FOOT (UPC) Associate lecturer at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa, specialising in communication and digital marketing. She drives the dissemination of results and communication with users and professionals. CUV staff SLT UPC Language and Terminology Service Language quality Ensures correct usage, appropriate terminology and consistency in the Catalan used on the platform and in communication and transfer materials. Service website "}, {"u": "team.html#g-institucional", "p": "Team", "t": "Institutional support", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "Dra.", "x": "Institutional support Dra. Núria Vila Vidal Director of the CUV Optometrist · Associate professor, FOOT (UPC) Associate professor at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa and director of the University Vision Centre. FUTUR profile (UPC) EG Enric González López Technical director of the CUV Optometrist · Associate lecturer, FOOT (UPC) Associate lecturer at the Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa and technical director of the University Vision Centre. FUTUR profile (UPC) "}, {"u": "team.html#g-gestio", "p": "Team", "t": "Project management and support", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "ÁG Álex García Ortiz Project developer CUV and Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (UPC) More than twenty years of project management experience.", "x": "Project management and support ÁG Álex García Ortiz Project developer CUV and Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa (UPC) More than twenty years of project management experience. He contributes to planning, monitoring and coordinating the work streams and keeping the project on schedule. "}, {"u": "team.html#entitats", "p": "Team", "t": "Participating organisations", "z": "", "k": "team", "s": "University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Coordinating organisation.", "x": "Participating organisations University Vision Centre (CUV-UPC) Coordinating organisation. Faculty of Optics and Optometry of Terrassa, Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya. CUV website Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 Partner organisation. Lived experience and validation of the platform's social value. Association website Fundació Punt de Vista Partner organisation. Expertise in accessibility and inclusive design, and dissemination through its communication team. Foundation website Official College of Opticians and Optometrists of Catalonia (COOOC) Collaborator. The professional body for optics and optometry in Catalonia. It hosted the November 2026 co-creation sessions at its headquarters. COOOC website "}, {"u": "glossary.html", "p": "Glossary", "t": "Glossary", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Glossary of key low vision terms in plain language: visual acuity, visual field, AMD, glaucoma, visual rehabilitation, aids and technology.", "x": "Glossary of key low vision terms in plain language: visual acuity, visual field, AMD, glaucoma, visual rehabilitation, aids and technology. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-A", "p": "Glossary", "t": "A", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "AMD (age-related macular degeneration) An age-related disease of the macula, the central part of the retina.", "x": "A AMD (age-related macular degeneration) An age-related disease of the macula, the central part of the retina. It affects central vision (reading, recognising faces), while peripheral vision is usually preserved. Assistive technology for sight loss Techniques, devices and software that make technology usable for people with sight loss: screen readers and magnifiers, braille displays, electronic magnifiers and so on (known in Spanish and Catalan as 'tiflotecnología'). "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-B", "p": "Glossary", "t": "B", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Braille display A device connected to a computer or phone that shows on-screen text in braille, using pins that move up and down.", "x": "B Braille display A device connected to a computer or phone that shows on-screen text in braille, using pins that move up and down. Used mainly by people who are blind or have very little sight. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-C", "p": "Glossary", "t": "C", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Charles Bonnet syndrome Seeing things that are not there (shapes, patterns, people or landscapes) in people with significant sight loss.", "x": "C Charles Bonnet syndrome Seeing things that are not there (shapes, patterns, people or landscapes) in people with significant sight loss. It is not a mental illness: it is the brain's response to missing visual input. It is worth mentioning to the eye-care professional. Co-creation Designing a resource or service together with the people who will use it. In this project, people with low vision, professionals and organisations take part. Contrast sensitivity The ability to tell an object from its background when the difference in brightness is small. It matters a great deal in daily life: seeing steps, faces or text on grey paper. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-D", "p": "Glossary", "t": "D", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Diabetic retinopathy Damage to the blood vessels of the retina caused by diabetes.", "x": "D Diabetic retinopathy Damage to the blood vessels of the retina caused by diabetes. Regular eye examinations and good diabetes control help prevent it from progressing. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-E", "p": "Glossary", "t": "E", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Easy read A way of writing and presenting text so that more people can understand it: short sentences, everyday words and ideas in a clear order.", "x": "E Easy read A way of writing and presenting text so that more people can understand it: short sentences, everyday words and ideas in a clear order. Eccentric viewing A technique of looking slightly to one side so that a healthy area of the retina, outside the damaged centre, is used. It is learned in visual rehabilitation. Electronic magnifier A device with a camera and screen that enlarges text and objects and lets you change colours and contrast. There are portable and desktop models (video magnifiers or CCTVs). "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-F", "p": "Glossary", "t": "F", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Filter lenses A tinted lens that blocks part of the light (often short-wavelength light) to reduce glare and improve perceived contrast.", "x": "F Filter lenses A tinted lens that blocks part of the light (often short-wavelength light) to reduce glare and improve perceived contrast. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-G", "p": "Glossary", "t": "G", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Glare Discomfort or loss of vision caused by too much light or by reflections.", "x": "G Glare Discomfort or loss of vision caused by too much light or by reflections. It is very common in low vision and can be reduced with filters, a visor or cap, and good lighting. Glaucoma A group of diseases that damage the optic nerve, often associated with raised eye pressure. It usually affects peripheral vision first and has no symptoms at the start, which is why regular check-ups matter. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-H", "p": "Glossary", "t": "H", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "High (pathological) myopia Very high short-sightedness (usually over 6 dioptres) that may be accompanied by damage to the retina and macula, retinal detachment or glaucoma.", "x": "H High (pathological) myopia Very high short-sightedness (usually over 6 dioptres) that may be accompanied by damage to the retina and macula, retinal detachment or glaucoma. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-I", "p": "Glossary", "t": "I", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Intraocular lens An artificial lens placed inside the eye, usually during cataract surgery, to replace the natural lens.", "x": "I Intraocular lens An artificial lens placed inside the eye, usually during cataract surgery, to replace the natural lens. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-L", "p": "Glossary", "t": "L", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Leber hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) A rare inherited disease that damages the optic nerve and causes rapid loss of central vision, often in young people.", "x": "L Leber hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) A rare inherited disease that damages the optic nerve and causes rapid loss of central vision, often in young people. Side vision is usually partly preserved. Legal blindness When vision is below limits set by law or by an organisation (for ONCE membership in Spain, visual acuity of 0.1 or less, or a visual field of 10 degrees or less, in the better eye with correction). Many legally blind people still have some sight. Low vision Vision loss that cannot be fully corrected with glasses, contact lenses, medication or surgery and that makes everyday tasks difficult, but where some useful residual vision remains. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-M", "p": "Glossary", "t": "M", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Macula The central part of the retina, responsible for detailed vision and much of our colour vision.", "x": "M Macula The central part of the retina, responsible for detailed vision and much of our colour vision. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-O", "p": "Glossary", "t": "O", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Optical low vision aid A device with lenses that makes the image larger: hand-held or stand magnifiers, illuminated magnifiers, high-addition spectacles or telescopes.", "x": "O Optical low vision aid A device with lenses that makes the image larger: hand-held or stand magnifiers, illuminated magnifiers, high-addition spectacles or telescopes. It should be chosen, and its use learned, with a professional. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-P", "p": "Glossary", "t": "P", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Peripheral vision The part of vision that sees to the sides, above and below while looking straight ahead.", "x": "P Peripheral vision The part of vision that sees to the sides, above and below while looking straight ahead. It is key to moving around without tripping. It is lost, for example, in advanced glaucoma and retinitis pigmentosa. Photophobia (light sensitivity) Discomfort or pain caused by light, making it hard to see in bright conditions. Selective filters, peaked caps and dark mode on screens can help. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-R", "p": "Glossary", "t": "R", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Rare disease A disease that affects few people (in Europe, fewer than 5 in 10,000).", "x": "R Rare disease A disease that affects few people (in Europe, fewer than 5 in 10,000). Many inherited diseases of the retina and optic nerve are rare diseases. Residual vision The vision a person still has. With aids, good lighting and training it can be used much more effectively. Retinitis pigmentosa A group of inherited retinal diseases. It often starts with difficulty seeing at night and a gradual narrowing of the peripheral visual field. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-S", "p": "Glossary", "t": "S", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Scotoma An area of the visual field where vision is missing or reduced, surrounded by areas of preserved vision.", "x": "S Scotoma An area of the visual field where vision is missing or reduced, surrounded by areas of preserved vision. It can be central (for example in AMD) or peripheral. Screen magnifier Software that enlarges part of a computer or phone screen. Operating systems include one (Windows Magnifier, Apple Zoom, Android Magnification) and there are dedicated products such as ZoomText. Screen reader Software that reads the screen content aloud (or shows it in braille). Examples include NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver and TalkBack. Sighted guide technique A safe way of guiding a person with sight loss: they hold your arm just above the elbow while you walk half a step ahead and point out obstacles. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-V", "p": "Glossary", "t": "V", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "Visual acuity The eye's ability to see fine detail.", "x": "V Visual acuity The eye's ability to see fine detail. It is measured with charts of letters or symbols of decreasing size and recorded, for example, as a decimal value (1.0 is the usual reference), a Snellen fraction or logMAR. Visual field The whole area you can see while looking straight at one point, including peripheral (side) vision. Visual rehabilitation Training, guided by professionals, to make the best use of residual vision: reading techniques, eccentric viewing, using aids and strategies for daily life. "}, {"u": "glossary.html#lletra-W", "p": "Glossary", "t": "W", "z": "", "k": "glossary", "s": "WCAG The W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines.", "x": "W WCAG The W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines. They define how to make websites usable by everyone, including people with visual or hearing impairments. White cane A cane used by people with visual impairment to detect obstacles and to let others know they see poorly. Many people with low vision use it only at certain times, for example at night. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "Accessibility", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "Accessibility statement for Baixa visió en xarxa: standards, adaptation tools, assistive technology support, hearing accessibility and how to report problems.", "x": "Accessibility statement for Baixa visió en xarxa: standards, adaptation tools, assistive technology support, hearing accessibility and how to report problems. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html#compromis", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "Our commitment", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "Our aim is to meet level AA of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 and, wherever possible, level AAA .", "x": "Our commitment Our aim is to meet level AA of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 and, wherever possible, level AAA . Accessibility is validated with people with low vision, with advice from the Associació Discapacitat Visual Catalunya: B1+B2+B3 and the Fundació Punt de Vista . This statement will be updated with the results of user testing. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html#eines", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "Tools to adapt the website", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "At the top of every page there is an always-visible accessibility bar with the most requested options: Text A− / A+: text size from 100% to 300%.", "x": "Tools to adapt the website At the top of every page there is an always-visible accessibility bar with the most requested options: Text A− / A+: text size from 100% to 300%. Icons grow with the text without distorting, and blocks reflow without overlapping. Contrast: quick switch between a light background (black on white) and a dark background (white on black), in both directions. Listen to this page: a built-in reader that reads the page aloud, designed for public computers or for anyone without a screen reader installed. More options opens the full panel: seven colour schemes: automatic; light background (light, black on white, black on yellow); dark background (dark, white on black, yellow on black); line spacing and letter and word spacing, each with three levels; highlighted links, reading guide, large cursor and greyscale; stopping animations and motion; read-aloud with pause and adjustable speed. The guides in the family area also have a Listen to this guide button. Preferences are only saved in your browser. No personal data is collected. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html#disseny", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "How it is designed", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "Contrast: all text and background combinations have a contrast ratio of at least 7:1.", "x": "How it is designed Contrast: all text and background combinations have a contrast ratio of at least 7:1. System contrast modes: the website respects Windows, macOS and mobile high-contrast modes without hiding icons, symbols or separators. Typeface: Atkinson Hyperlegible, created by the Braille Institute to make reading easier for people with low vision. The base size is 18 pixels. Zoom: the website adapts to small screens and can be zoomed to 400% without losing content or scrolling horizontally. Keyboard: everything can be done with the keyboard. Focus is highly visible and there is a link to skip straight to the content. Screen readers and braille: landmarks (header, navigation, main content, footer) and sequential h1 to h3 headings without gaps, checked automatically by the publishing system. Tested with VoiceOver, TalkBack, NVDA and braille displays. Images: all informative images have hand-written alternative text, without relying on automatic AI tools. Videos: no video is published without captions, audio description and a full transcript. Two clicks: any section can be reached from the home page in one or two clicks, and the resource assistant goes straight to the content. Not just colour: the three areas are also distinguished by name, number and icon. Language: plain language and “In a nutshell” summaries. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html#auditiva", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "Hearing accessibility", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "All information on the website is available as text.", "x": "Hearing accessibility All information on the website is available as text. Website notifications (for example, when you change a setting) are shown visually and also announced to screen readers. Videos and audio will have synchronised captions, audio description and a full transcript. Catalan Sign Language (LSC) interpretation will be considered for the main content. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html#navegador", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "Tips for your device", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "Zoom the page: Ctrl and + (Windows and Linux) or Cmd and + (Mac).", "x": "Tips for your device Zoom the page: Ctrl and + (Windows and Linux) or Cmd and + (Mac). To return to normal size: Ctrl and 0 . Mobile: in your phone's accessibility settings you can enlarge text and turn on magnification and the screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android). Reader mode: many browsers have a reader mode that shows only the main text. "}, {"u": "accessibility.html#incidencies", "p": "Accessibility", "t": "Found a barrier?", "z": "", "k": "accessibility", "s": "If any part of this website is not accessible to you, please tell us through the feedback form or contact the University Vision Centre.", "x": "Found a barrier? If any part of this website is not accessible to you, please tell us through the feedback form or contact the University Vision Centre. You will help us improve it. Report a problem "}];
